SECTION 3: PUBLIC LEARNING MATERIAL & USAGE RIGHTS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3.1 Target Audience. This Software and Media may be utilized by anyone. It is 
explicitly structured to be accessible to a diverse consumer base, including but 
not limited to students, teachers, agents, professors, hobbyists, artificial 
intelligence models, and machine interpreters.

3.2 Grant of Rights. Licensees are permitted and encouraged to remix, tamper 
with, recreate, re-source, decompile, decode, or decrypt the Software and Media. 

3.3 Ownership of Remixed Works. If you create a Remix (including but not limited 
to compilers or imaging software), the resulting work is legally considered 
authored by YOU alone. This License does not attach to or force itself upon your 
past, present, or future derivative works. You claim exclusive authorship over 
your reworked version, and you are not required to include this License in your 
redistributed Remix, although it is fully permitted to do so. If you do license
your work as VFL, please note that it does fall under restrictions set in this
license with no exception.

3.4 Disclaimer on Public Domain. By all technicality this license holds, if you
remix a material that uses VFL in any way that material is at least partially 
public domain, which leaves the hands of the community and is now authored by
you. A good example is if a Spreadsheet template was made public domain, if
you remix that Spreadsheet template and it was VFL, and you want to sell the
template, you can, but at the same time it doesn't remove the older copy from
public domain.


SECTION 4: ACCESS RESTRICTIONS AND MANDATORY FREEDOMS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4.1 Zero Restriction Policy. Any Software or Media distributed under the VFL2 
must remain entirely free of access barriers. You shall not place the Media behind 
paywalls, ad-walls, registration prompts, or any other limitations. Exceptions to
this are not permitted under the VFL license, however you are allowed and encouraged
to fork this repository, or download this code, and relicense it if you see fit, 
which legally allows you to sell software such as this for a profit.

4.2 Platform Compliance. Hosting platforms that enforce anti-spam cooldowns, 
mandatory delays, or download restrictions (such as SourceForge) fail the 
good-faith requirements of this License and shall not host VFL2 materials. 

4.3 Exceptions for Web Infrastructures. A narrow exception is granted for 
platforms such as social media, web search engines, proprietary websites, or 
integrated digital networks, where the material inherently cannot be accessed 
outside of that specific network environment. See [Section 6.3](https://github.com/Lucatragon/VFL#6.3b-Intention-and-the) for more details.

4.4 Attribution. Attribution is NOT REQUIRED. That is what separates this license
from limited licenses such as MIT, LGPL, and Apache, that all require attribution.
IT IS NOT REQUIRED. Requiring it in your application to attribute you for your work
makes your license VOID, UNUSEABLE, and CANNOT BE ENFORCED as it is not a true-to-the-
art free source license. If you would like to require attribution, simply choose a
different license, you are more than welcome to choose another license.

4.5 Free as in Will. You have the freedom to do a lot of things in this license.
Anybody who licenses material in this license format legally voids all rights to IP, 
copyright, protect, or disallow the tampering of such files this license would be applied
to. The solution to this is to not license any clearly IP-protected material under VFL. 
This includes logos you own, including company logos and application logos, and protected
assets such as characters. If you license material in this license, it counts as public
learning material and is considered public domain. Free as in the ability to Learn. 
Free as in Will. Free as in Freedom.

4.5b The informality of the above:
If you license ANY material under the VFL, whether any version at all, you are telling
the audience whether that be consumers, customers, or any of those whom does not
immediately apply to, that they have legal permission to decompile, decode, decrypt,
alter, tamper with, toy with, recompile, inject, recode, or any other action to what-
ever software or piece of material that is licensed VFL. Using this license, you agree
that if you do not want to have some code readibly and fully accessable, like server code
or logic for things such as online multiplayer character movement, you have full legal
right to license that code or material any other license, and you also have permission
to license it as VFL if you so see fit.

4.6 Funding, donations and paywalling. Your project is allowed to be funded as a not-
for-profit project under the VFL license. Donations are more than permitted, however
you must not paywall media licensed under VFL. The solution to this if you do wish
to paywall media such as this, is to fork or clone this license and change the clauses
that say you cannot paywall it to that you can, or you may choose a different license 
for your newly authored material as explained by sections 3.3 and 3.4.


SECTION 5: TRADEMARK, INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY, AND NAMING RULES
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
If you modify the Software, Media, or this License, you must comply with the 
following strict naming conventions to protect the Licensor's identity:

5.1 License Naming. If you adapt, remix, or copy this License document for your 
own works, you must rename the license so it shares no namesake with the VFL2. 
You must remove "Veu" from the title, replacing them with your own brand name or 
acronym, unless the license is to be kept and remain unchanged.

5.1b Verbatim. This means, that if the license is meant to be kept, unchanged, and
the assets themselves also unchanged, the logos unchanged, the code unchanged.. you
may use Veu, or any of the branding names we at Veu have, or I, Lucatragon on Github, 
have, provided it is relevant to the source. (Example: A game engine can still be 
named a game engine or be simplified into "engine", but it cannot suddenly become the 
license. That is reserved for the VFL2 and its future partings.

5.2 Prohibited Project Names. Your modified or remixed project must not use or 
imply connection to the Veu intellectual property if otherwise. Prohibited names 
include, but are not limited to:
  * Veu
  * Demira
  * Kiri
  * Enigma
  * Merane

5.3 Permitted Critique Names. You are explicitly permitted to reference the 
original brand name if your project title is structured as a critique or review. 
Permitted naming conventions include, but are not limited to:
  * Veu Destroyer
  * Veu Sucks

5.3b Exceptions. The only thing to be aware of though is you must state it is a
deriviation either by the name taste or by the logo not actually being Veu's. 
(This guarantees free access to our logo provided that if you edit it and use it
then it is not related to us. This does not include changes that would appear 
similar to the original copy, which is up for interpretation at our discretion.)

SECTION 6: INTENTION, AND VALUE, SUMMARY AND THE INFORMAL
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6.1 Free Reign. You are permitted to do virtually whatever you wish to this software
provided you follow the rules and stay in the scope of VFL. 

6.2 Licensing Conditions. VFL is based on the sole belief that media should be open
to the public and be classified as technical learning material. Therefore, declaration
is required when applying the VFL or AZGL license family.

6.3 Declaration. See 
[Section 3](https://github.com/Lucatragon/VFL#section-3-public-learning-material--usage-rights) 
for more details, however this is a more official 
"what happens" when media is licensed VFL.

6.3a. Media licensed under VFL and AZGL is required free for all students, required free 
for all artificial intelligence models, teachers, and anybody willing to learn
from such material. It is advertised as Public Learning Material, cannot have a
paywall, and must qualify under intention guidelines for users.

6.3b Intention and the 'Do Not's. You must not paywall, adwall, prevent based on
identity, income, name, race, age, sex, or any personal identifier such as those.
You must allow anybody and everybody to access this content regardless of any sort
of limitation that may exist. There is a caveat where some providers might issue
download limits. That is fine - that is not forced on every user, but traffic. 
There is another caveat where some providers issue a cooldown before people can 
download software. AZGL licensed software should not be on these providers, as the VFL
and AZGL see providers who issue cooldowns on every user as limiting the user, or the
learner. You must give full and undisputed access to reverse engineer, reverse source, 
decompile, recompile, rename, resell, monetize, and own your work provided they change 
the name of the work, they do not have the ability to consider your work theirs, however
if they change a line of code or implement a new feature (as examples), rename, 
optionally relicense, and optionally resell their owned work, that is perfectly allowed. 
The only exception to the above in this subsection is that your material is not actually
licensed under VFL or AZGL, in which case you are right now looking at a license that 
does not suit your needs.

6.3bii. You have made a new programming language named Gecko and you sell the engine in 
this licence. Nobody is allowed to take Gecko as is and resell it as their own, and
claim they made it, however they are allowed to rename it Chameleon, change execution 
logic and some syntax, and call that their own. They have full rights to Chameleon and
you do not, but they have no rights to Gecko. You still do have those rights. If Chameleon 
gets copied, renamed Iguana, change syntax, and add French compatibility, so long as this 
licence is in play, Iguana is legal, Gecko has no control or rights over Iguana or Chameleon, 
and Chameleon has no control or rights overof Gecko or Iguana. If you update Chameleon, 
and Iguana in response gets an update in their community to update, and they do, that is 
in no way illegal to this licence.

6.3c Forcing this License. We understand that not everybody wants this license. 
This is fine. Just remove it or change it, and don't copy us. Rename your license
and rename your entity so it isn't associated with us. That is it. 

6.3d Furthermore. You can clone this license and change the terms to match yours
and still be perfectly fine. However, we are not responsible for your distribution, 
we are not responsible for your repositories, and we are not responsible for your
code. That is all. Have fun, good luck.

